WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.320
- [Instructor] So let's
see if we can come up ways

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:07.320
to compute what 2.8 times 4.73 is.

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.650
So pause this video,
and try to work it out.

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:11.010
And, actually, I'll give you a hint.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:13.210
Try to figure out, just using the digits,

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:15.430
not even paying attention to the decimals,

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:17.610
the digits that the product would have,

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:19.300
and then use estimation to think about

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:21.860
where to place the decimal in your product

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:24.100
so you get a reasonable answer.

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:25.850
All right, now, let's do this together.

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:27.590
So let's just imagine
that we were multiplying

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:29.110
these numbers without decimals.

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:30.360
So that would be a situation

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:35.360
where we would have 473 times 28,

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.700
and so we could try to compute that.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:40.430
And, so, we could think about
let's multiply everything

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:44.010
times the eight, so
three times eight is 24,

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:48.430
seven times eight is 56, plus two is 58.

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:52.480
And then four times eight
is 32, plus five is 37.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:54.590
And then we could multiply
everything times the two.

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:56.630
I'll cross those out so
I don't get confused.

00:00:56.630 --> 00:00:59.830
Three times two is going to be six,

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:01.230
so we have to be very careful,

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:04.100
we are now in the tens place,
so we want a zero here,

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:08.500
so three times two tens
is going to be six tens.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.520
Seven times two is 14.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:15.530
Four times two is eight, plus one is nine.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:18.000
We add everything together,

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.090
and we get four plus zero is four,

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:22.220
eight plus six is 14,

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:25.770
and then one plus seven plus four is 12,

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:29.080
and then we get one plus three is four,

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:32.150
plus nine is equal to 13.

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:35.950
So we know that the final
answer has the digits one,

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:38.660
three, two, four, four in that order.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:42.730
One, three, two, four, four.

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:45.200
So now we have to think about
where would we put a decimal

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:46.820
for this to be a reasonable answer?

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:48.720
And here's where estimation is useful.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:52.580
We know that 2.8 times 4.73

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.950
is going to be roughly equal to what?

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:57.770
Well, 2.8 is pretty close to three,

00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:00.950
so I'll estimate 2.8 as being three.

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:05.060
And 4.73 is, if I had to estimate,

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:07.540
I would say, "Hey, it'd
be pretty close to five."

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:09.730
So this should be pretty
close to three times five,

00:02:09.730 --> 00:02:12.090
so it should be close to 15.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:14.170
So if I were to put the decimal there,

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:17.160
that's way more than 15, so
that doesn't seem reasonable.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:18.800
Even if I were to put the decimal there,

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:22.230
1,324.4 is still way more than 15.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:23.590
If I were to put the decimal there,

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:25.410
still way more than 15.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:26.870
If I were to put the decimal there,

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:28.880
hey, that actually feels about right.

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:33.720
13 and 244 thousandths is,

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:37.010
is approaching 15, it's in the ballpark.

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:38.660
And it's actually the closest,

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:40.070
'cause if we were to
put the decimal there,

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:44.000
then we go to 1.3244,
so a lot less than 15.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.820
So if we want this to
be roughly equal to 15,

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:50.040
we definitely would wanna put
the decimal right over there.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:54.630
That is the most reasonable
computation we can do,

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:55.770
'cause we know the digits

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:58.230
are going to be one,
three, two, four, four,

00:02:58.230 --> 00:02:59.970
and this helps us put the decimal.

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:02.060
In the future, we're
gonna come up with ways

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:03.270
of doing it that where
you don't necessarily

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:04.930
have to estimate, but I encourage you,

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:07.010
the estimation is always key.

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:09.680
If you ever in your life
forget some type of method

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:11.740
or process for multiplying decimals,

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:15.010
it's the estimation that
allows you to understand

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:18.020
whether you're coming up
with a reasonable answer.

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:19.460
This is really important,

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:21.030
'cause the decimal, there's a,

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:23.430
I remember reading a news
story a couple years ago

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:27.050
where someone put in a stock trade

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:28.620
where they got the decimal wrong.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:31.950
And because of that, they
essentially sold 10 times

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:33.630
as many shares as they were supposed to,

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:35.940
and so they lost hundreds
of millions of dollars.

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:37.723
So, anyway, decimals are important.

